WW-DHSVM
========

Author and maintainer
---------------------

Zhi Li <zhi.2.li@uconn.edu>
  University of Connecticut
  https://github.com/ZhiLiHydro

Maintained under https://github.com/UConn-EFC.


Inherited work
--------------

WW-DHSVM is a fork of Watershed Workflow v2.1.0.  A substantial part of
this repository is that project's work rather than ours -- in particular
the whole of ww_dhsvm/sources/ (the source-manager architecture and the
data-source implementations for WBD, NHD, 3DEP, NLCD, NRCS/SSURGO,
SoilGrids, POLARIS, Pelletier, GLHYMPS, Daymet, AORC, MODIS/AppEEARS,
HyRiver, hf_hydrodata and BASIN-3D), together with crs.py, warp.py,
colors.py, dynamic_colormaps.py, scalebar.py, io.py, utils.py, data.py
and their companions.

That code remains under its original 3-clause BSD licence, reproduced in
LICENSE.watershed-workflow.  Credit for it belongs to:

  Ethan T. Coon, Pin Shuai, and the Watershed Workflow contributors
  https://github.com/environmental-modeling-workflows/watershed-workflow

  Coon, E. T., & Shuai, P. (2022).  Watershed Workflow: A toolset for
  parameterizing data-intensive, integrated hydrologic models.
  Environmental Modelling & Software, 157, 105502.


Models and data this tool depends on
------------------------------------

DHSVM is developed at Pacific Northwest National Laboratory and is not
redistributed here; see https://github.com/pnnl/DHSVM-PNNL.

  Wigmosta, M. S., Vail, L. W., & Lettenmaier, D. P. (1994).  A
  distributed hydrology-vegetation model for complex terrain.  Water
  Resources Research, 30(6), 1665-1679.

  Perkins, W. A., et al. (2019).  Environmental Modelling & Software,
  122, 104533.  (the parallel branch)

Data products -- USGS WBD, NHDPlus and 3DEP; MRLC NLCD; POLARIS; NOAA
AORC and HRRR -- are downloaded at run time and are not redistributed
here.  Each carries its own licence and citation requirements.
